/ref 1033 87 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051079 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7f29909c-8f45-5a17-820a-13a0a327cd5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186096843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1031 "The Chain Rule for Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1033 1108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1034 1.4416972363891276 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051084 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7fcc3a06-676a-55a2-9979-d3726053babe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054407 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12994" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054407 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+1)^2(x^2 -x -2)(x-2)(x+3)$ has multiplicity $3$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7dee7aa-bbb9-50ba-9b0c-6aa0fecbe49d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054407 #edb/ref 1074 "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's completely factor $P(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x) &= (x + 1)^{2}(x^{2} - x - 2)(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{2}((x + 1)(x - 2))(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{2}(x + 1)(x - 2)(x - 2)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 1)^{3}(x - 2)^{2}(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $P(x)$ are $x=-1$, $x=2$, and $x=-3$.\n\n- The factor $(x+1)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=-1$ has multiplicity $3$.\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $2$.\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $P(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1$ (and it is a simple root).\n\nTherefore, the root with multiplicity $3$ is $x=-1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054408 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12995" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054408 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ in the polynomial $p(x) = 2x^3 + 13x^2 + 24x + 9$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f83ec339-8cf8-5786-a17e-063842ef9a8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054408 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x)$, the factor theorem tells us that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$.\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x)$, we know that $(x+3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x)$, we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-3$ | $2$ | $13$ | $24$ | $9$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ | $-21$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p(x)}{x+3} = 2x^2 + 7x + 3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x) = (x+3) (2x^2 + 7x + 3)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= (x + 3)(2x + 1)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + 3)^{2}(2x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054409 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054409 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x - 12$ have multiplicity $1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4600595a-b472-56e5-bce1-e57102880761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054409 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= x^{3} + 3x^{2} - 4x - 12 \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x + 3) - 4(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 4)(x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(x + 2)(x + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $1$.\n- The factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $1$.\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1$.\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=2,-2,-3$ all have multiplicity $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054410 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12997" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054410 #edb/ref 1071 "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-5x^2$ have multiplicity $1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054410 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c9da6c44-61e0-5b73-b1b4-fa9e4f0ac440" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054410 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to factor the polynomial.\n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $x^3$ and $-5x^2$ is $x^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(x) &= x^{3} - 5x^{2} \\\\\n&= x^{2}(x - 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x^2$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2$.\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x)$. So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1$.\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054455 #edb/ref 1069 "e-134" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054455 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ when $f(x)=x^3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054455 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054455 #edb/ref 1074 "The power rule states that if\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3\n$$\n\nwe have $n=3$. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3 \\cdot x^{3 - 1} = 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054456 #edb/ref 1069 "e-135" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054456 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054456 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054456 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, we can rewrite the function as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (-2) \\cdot x^{-2 - 1} \\\\\n&=-2x^{-3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054457 #edb/ref 1069 "e-136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054457 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ when $y=\\sqrt{x}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054457 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054457 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{1/2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054465 #edb/ref 1069 "e-137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054465 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 12$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054465 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, $f'(x)=0$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054479 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054479 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the fifth term in the Fibonacci sequence of numbers, defined by\n$a_{n+2}=a_{n+1} + a_n, \\quad a_1 = a_2 = 1, \\quad n \\geq 1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054479 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c861e757-63e3-5ad3-baf2-83603cc930bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054479 #edb/ref 1074 "The fifth term of the sequence corresponds to $a_5$.\n\nTo compute $a_5$, we first need to compute $a_3$. So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n + 2} &= a_{n + 1} + a_{n} \\\\\na_{1 + 2} &= a_{1 + 1} + a_{1} \\\\\na_{3} &= a_{2} + a_{1} \\\\\na_{3} &= 1 + 1 \\\\\na_{3} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $a_4$, we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{2 + 2} &= a_{2 + 1} + a_{2} \\\\\na_{4} &= a_{3} + a_{2} \\\\\na_{4} &= 2 + 1 \\\\\na_{4} &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe continue this process to compute $a_5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{5} &= a_{4} + a_{3} = 3 + 2 = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fifth term of the sequence is $a_5 = 5$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054480 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14376" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054480 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the fourth term of the sequence\n\n$f(n+2)=f(n+1)+f(n) \\,, \\quad f(1)=-1\\,, \\quad f(2) = 3, \\quad n \\geq 1$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50fbf2eb-7e2d-51f6-ac24-378afac328c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054480 #edb/ref 1074 "The fourth term is represented by $f(4)$.\n\nTo compute $f(4)$, we first need to compute $f(3)$. So, we substitute $n=1$ into the recursive formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(n+2) & =f(n+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(1+2) & =f(1+1)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & = f(2)+f(1) \\\\ f(3) & = 3+(-1) \\\\ f(3)&= 2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to compute $f(4)$, we substitute $n=2$ into the recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} f(2+2) & =f(2+1)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & = f(3)+f(2) \\\\ f(4) & = 2+3 \\\\ f(4)&= 5 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term of the sequence is\n\n$$\nf(4)=5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054497 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7f/7ff4e40e3a505af29013dded0c58b2bde88bc826d612e68774034ef288309ec8.png)\n\nA gym personal trainer recorded the duration of an exercise session for a group of $10$ gym visitors. The results are presented above in the form of a histogram. Find an estimate of the mean duration of these exercise sessions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e476b6a9-88bd-5897-8c0c-bd8859a5f18b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054497 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the mean can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline x \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot m_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class, and\n- $n$ is the total number of observations.\n\nTo estimate the mean, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each class and the products of the midpoints and frequencies.\n\n| Duration ($\\textrm{min}$) | $m_i$ | $f_i$ | $m_i \\cdot f_i$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $40 \\leq d \\lt 50$ | $45$ | $4$ | $180$ |\n| $50 \\leq d \\lt 60$ | $55$ | $2$ | $110$ |\n| $60 \\leq d \\lt 70$ | $65$ | $3$ | $195$ |\n| $70 \\leq d \\lt 80$ | $75$ | $1$ | $75$ |\n\nFinally, we sum the values in the last column and divide by $10$ (the total number of observations).\n\n$$\nd^(―) \\mid \\approx \\frac{180 + 110 + 195 + 75}{10}; = \\frac{560}{10}; = 56min\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean duration of these exercise sessions is approximately $56\\,\\textrm{min}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054498 #edb/ref 1069 "e-16512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054498 #edb/ref 1071 "A retailer tracks the weekly sales of a popular product for several years. The number of units sold per week is recorded in the frequency table. Estimate the standard deviation of the sales data.\n\n| Units sold ($x_i$) | Frequency $(f_i)$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $10 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $30$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 30$ | $20$ |\n| $30 \\leq x \\lt 40$ | $50$ |\n\nGiven that an estimate of the mean weekly sale is $\\overline x \\approx 27$ units per week, find an estimate for the standard deviation $\\sigma_n$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96d65347-9223-549a-b214-30942fecc794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054498 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, the variance can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2_n \\approx \\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^K f_i \\cdot (m_{i} - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f_i$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n- $m_i$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n- $\\overline x$ is the mean.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| $x_i$ | $f_i$ | $m_i$ | $\\left(m_i - \\overline x\\right)^2$ | $f_i \\left(m_i - \\overline x\\right)^2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $10 \\leq x \\lt 20$ | $30$ | $15$ | $144$ | $4320$ |\n| $20 \\leq x \\lt 30$ | $20$ | $25$ | $4$ | $80$ |\n| $30\\leq x \\lt 40$ | $50$ | $35$ | $64$ | $3200$ |\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by the total number of observations:\n\n$$\nσ_{n}^{2} \\mid \\approx \\frac{4320 + 80 + 3200}{30 + 20 + 50}; = \\frac{7600}{100}; = 76\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nσ_{n} &= \\sqrt{76} \\approx 8.7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to one decimal place, the standard deviation is $8.7$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054522 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3912" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054522 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x) = 2x - 3$ and $g(x) = x + 6$, then $(fg)(x)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a523530-ec38-5df8-997f-2f94975eb87b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054522 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $(fg)(x)$ means the product of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\nTo find the product of the two functions $f$ and $g$, we multiply them together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (fg)(x) &= f(x)\\cdot g(x) \\\\[3pt] &= (2x - 3)(x + 6) \\\\[3pt] &= 2x^2 + 12x -3x - 18 \\\\[3pt] &= 2x^2 + 9x - 18 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054523 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054523 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $(f/g)(-1)$ for $f(x) = 2x +5$ and $g(x) = x^3+2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79c8f0a9-6f05-515f-abaa-553863402a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054523 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $(f/g)(x)$ means the quotient of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f/g)(-1)$, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$, and then divide the results:\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 2(-1) + 5 = 3 \\\\\ng(-1) &= (-1)^{3} + 2 = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the results:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(-1) = \\dfrac{f(-1)}{g(-1)} = \\dfrac{3}{1} =3\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f/g)(x)$ by dividing $f$ by $g$:\n\n$$\n(f/g)(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{x^{3} + 2}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f/g)(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f/g)(-1) &= (2(-1) + 5)/((-1)^{3} + 2) = \\frac{3}{1} = 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054524 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3914" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054524 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $f(x) = 2x + 7$ and $g(x) = 5x - 24$, find $(f - g)(x)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c68e70d0-ccec-578a-96c1-7ed106aebc79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054524 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $(f-g)(x)$ means the difference of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\nTo find the difference of the two functions, we subtract $g(x)$ from $f(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (f - g)(x) &= f(x) - g(x) \\\\[3pt] &=(2x + 7) - (5x - 24) \\\\[3pt] &= (2x-5x) + (7-(-24)) \\\\[3pt] &= -3x+31 \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054533 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054533 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $2 \\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a1183fe-8fdc-549e-8f1b-4b0d6b7909aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054533 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i}\n\t= (2\\cdot 3) \\,\\textrm{i}\n\t= 6\\,\\textrm{i}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054534 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054534 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $2\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot (-3\\,\\textrm{i}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054534 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb4ef9ac-0c5d-5fab-b84f-c2bad182b3c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054534 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. We also use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (-3\\,\\text{i}) & =2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054535 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054535 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $6\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot(4 + 2\\,\\textrm{i}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8f53a72e-adc4-584d-8d51-efb42c3ddbc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054535 #edb/ref 1074 "To multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number, we use the distributive property and the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (4+2\\,\\text{i}) & =6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot 4+6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\,\\text{i}+6\\cdot 2\\cdot \\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{i}+12\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{i}-12 \\\\  & =-12+24\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054536 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054536 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $(1 - 3\\,\\textrm{i})(2 + 4\\, \\textrm{i}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c3412df7-29a2-50d3-be4d-e2c6fcf8804a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054536 #edb/ref 1074 "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\,\\text{i})(2+4\\,\\text{i}) & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4\\,\\text{i}+(-3\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 2+(-3\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 4\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+4\\,\\text{i}-6\\,\\text{i}-12\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\,\\text{i}-12\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-2\\,\\text{i}+12 \\\\  & =14-2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054540 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054540 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate $\\log_{16}(32)$ without using a calculator." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc85eeb4-dbc0-5ea7-af3a-60270d6ae722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054540 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=16$ and $X =32$. Since both $a$ and $X$ are powers of $2$, we set $b=2$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{16} (32) &= (\\log_{2} (32))/(\\log_{2} (16)) \\\\\n&= (\\log_{2} (2^{5}))/(\\log_{2} (2^{4})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054541 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4091" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054541 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\log x$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} x} {\\log_{2} 10}$\n2. $\\dfrac {\\log_{2} 10} {\\log_{2} x}$\n3. $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{x}{10}\\right)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15844e53-9573-5e6f-8fef-debbe0a142ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054541 #edb/ref 1074 "The change of base formula for logarithms is\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=10$ to base $b=2$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log x &= \\log_{10} x \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\log_{2} x}{\\log_{2} 10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054543 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4100" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054543 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a calculator to evaluate $\\log_3 (7)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "890afebc-d130-5dbc-aaf7-c2cd4db193f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054543 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's calculate $\\log_3 (7)$ by changing the base $3$ to the common logarithm (base $10$).\n\nWe use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X = \\dfrac {\\log_b X} {\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=3$, $X =7$, and $b=10$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_{3} (7) &= (\\log_{10} (7))/(\\log_{10} (3)) \\\\\n&= (\\log (7))/(\\log (3)) \\\\\n&\\approx \\frac{0.84510}{0.47712} \\\\\n&\\approx 1.77\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054544 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054544 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $(f + g)(-1)$ for $f(x) = 1 -2x$ and $g(x) = 4x + 3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6a4d0a8-f579-5d6d-ad74-59b68d2046e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054544 #edb/ref 1074 "The notation $(f+g)(x)$ means the sum of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo evaluate $(f + g)(-1)$, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$, and then add the results.\n\nFirst, we find $f(-1)$ and $g(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 1 - 2(-1) = 3 \\\\\ng(-1) &= 4(-1) + 3 =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we add the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(-1) &= f(-1) + g(-1) \\\\\n&= 3 + (-1) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFirst, we find $(f+g)(x)$ by adding the two functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(x) &= (1 - 2x) + (4x + 3) \\\\\n&= 2x + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate $(f + g)(-1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(f + g)(-1) &= -2 + 4 = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054548 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054548 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\left|z\\right|$ if $z=3 - 4\\textrm{i}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98b156f1-66b9-56f5-be59-ba15d1b857db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054548 #edb/ref 1074 "For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=a+b\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\n$$\n\nFor the complex number\n\n$$\nz=3-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe have $a=3$ and $b=-4$. So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (-4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054549 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054549 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the magnitude of the number $\\textrm{i}\\sqrt{2}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9f8cffb-bbf9-5b42-bec1-26c43277a335" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054549 #edb/ref 1074 "The imaginary number $\\textrm{i} \\sqrt{2}$ is equivalent to the complex number\n\n$$\nz = 0 + \\sqrt{2} \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nWe know that for a complex number\n\n$$\nz=a+b\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\n$$\n\nSo, its magnitude of\n\n$$\nz=0 + \\sqrt{2} \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{a^{2} + b^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(0)^{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{0 + 2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054550 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054550 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\left|z\\right|$ if $z=5c - 9d\\textrm{i}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb7cf016-642b-5cd2-8fd8-006b890b3c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054550 #edb/ref 1074 "For a complex number\n\n$$\nz=a+b\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nthe magnitude of $z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mid z \\mid = \\sqrt{a^2+b^2}\n$$\n\nFor the complex number\n\n$$\nz=5c - 9d\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nwe have $a=5c$ and $b=-9d$. So, the magnitude is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid z \\mid &= \\sqrt{(5c)^{2} + (-9d)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25c^{2} + 81d^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054567 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054567 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the probability that if a random $4$-digit number is created using the digits $1$ through $9$ (but *not* $0$), no digits are repeated?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054567 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b1fef9c-13b4-52c7-a7c0-c53cdc5dec7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054567 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{n}{N}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of $4$-digit numbers that can be created from the $9$ digits, and $n$ is the number of those $4$-digit numbers in which no digits are repeated.\n\nFirst, we compute $N$. We want to count how many $4$-digit numbers can be formed from a set of $9$ digits with repetition. This is equal to the number of permutations of $4$ items chosen from a set of $9$ with repetition, which is\n\n$$\nN = 9^4 = 6\\,561\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n$. To create a $4$-digit number from the $9$ digits where no digits are repeated, we choose $4$ of the $9$ possible digits. Since the order of the digits matters, we use permutations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn = _{9}P_{4} &= \\frac{9!}{5!} = 3024\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(A) &= \\frac{3024}{6561} \\approx 0.46 = 46%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
